### Read the title when the row is drawn

Suppose you're writing a to-do list. You want to draw the current title of
a task. The drawing function needs a way to get the text. The caller knows
where the task keeps it.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    Task = | id I32, title String |

    row_label |read_title || -> String| -> String {
        return [:Title: [read_title()]]
    }

    task ~= Task(42, "Draft")
    io.line(row_label(|| => task.title))
]

This prints `Title: Draft`. The parameter `read_title || -> String` declares
a *callback*. It tells the drawing function how to ask for a title. The caller
supplies a *closure*, `|| => task.title`. The empty pipes mean it takes no
arguments. It can still read `task` because the caller defined the closure
where `task` is visible. That use of an outer value is a *capture*.

`row_label` calls the closure immediately and returns a String. If the task's
title changes, call `row_label` again to draw the new text.

### Give each editor its own handler

A row editor can outlive the call that creates it. Give the editor a callback
field and declare `into return` on the factory parameter so the returned row
can keep its handler.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    EditMessage = { Rename | id I32, text String | }
    EditRow = | on_edit |text String| -> EditMessage |

    make_row |handler |text String| -> EditMessage into return| -> EditRow {
        return EditRow(handler)
    }

    apply_edit |target ~Task, change EditMessage| {
        if change is {
            case Rename(id, text) if id is target.id: ~target.title = text
            else:
        }
    }

    row_id = copy task.id
    row = make_row(|entered| => EditMessage::Rename(row_id, entered))

    change = row.on_edit("Ready for review")
    apply_edit(~task, change)
    io.line(row_label(|| => task.title))
]

When you build the row, you already know its ID. `copy task.id` takes that
ID once so the handler can use it without borrowing the task. The new text
arrives later as the argument `entered`. Calling the field produces
`EditMessage::Rename(42, "Ready for review")`. The application applies that
message and prints `Title: Ready for review`.

Calling `row.on_edit("Published")` would produce another message for row 42.
A browser adapter would have to deliver that input, and the application
would have to apply the message and draw again. Here the ordinary calls show
exactly when each step happens.
